You're on the right track!  Adding a Gaussian filter before Sobel is a common and effective technique to enhance edge detection by reducing noise. Here's the improved MATLAB code incorporating this step, along with explanations for maximum helpfulness and verbosity:

```matlab
% 1. Image Loading and Preprocessing
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image); 

% 2. Gaussian Smoothing (Noise Reduction)
%   - Choose a suitable sigma value (standard deviation) for your noise level. 
%     A common starting point is sigma = 1.5  
sigma = 1.5; 
gaussian_filter = fspecial('gaussian', [5 5], sigma); % Create Gaussian kernel
smoothed_image = imfilter(gray_image, gaussian_filter, 'replicate'); % Apply filtering

% 3. Sobel Operator Application
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1]; 
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];

edge_image_x = conv2(double(smoothed_image), sobel_x, 'same'); % X-direction derivative
edge_image_y = conv2(double(smoothed_image), sobel_y, 'same'); % Y-direction derivative

% 4. Edge Magnitude Calculation
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);  

% 5. Thresholding (Optional but Recommended)
%   -  Enhance contrast and clean up by applying a threshold.
threshold = 0.1 * max(edge_image(:));  % Adjust threshold as needed
binary_edges = edge_image > threshold;

% 6. Visualization
figure;
subplot(1,2,1); imshow(smoothed_image, []); title('Smoothed Image');
subplot(1,2,2); imshow(binary_edges, []); title('Edges (Thresholded)'); 
